A Linux window can show tabs of several engine profiles. Chromium needs a tab's Browser to have the tab's profile, so
the Ardia host keeps one hidden companion Browser per other profile beside the window's own (ac_linux_host.cc). A
companion's widget is never shown: BrowserView::Show, ShowInactive and Activate hand the request to the host, which
focuses the visible window instead, and Restore, Maximize, Minimize, SetBounds, Deactivate, FlashFrame and both the
synchronous and the asynchronous fullscreen paths (RequestFullscreen) are dropped, since
DesktopWindowTreeHostPlatform::Restore (and Maximize from minimized) show the window themselves. Tab-modal dialogs
and permission prompts of a companion's tabs are hosted by the visible window's BrowserView. A companion is not a
window of its own for closing: it raises no download or multi-tab warning of its own but still runs its tabs'
unload handlers through the normal close; UnloadController's last-window count skips it and the multi-tab warning
counts the whole window's tabs, so closing the only visible window asks once for the family; and neither its window
close nor the tabs it closes while closing (on quit too) reach TabRestoreService, so restoring never opens it as a
window. The hooks are null outside the Linux host and answer null, false or -1 for every browser that is not a
companion or a host.

